T("Step 8: An Inventory class",
  "A bare list of products works, but every operation (add, find, total) ends up as loose code scattered around. Wrap the list in an Inventory class: it **has** products (composition) and offers methods for everything you do with them.",
  "class Inventory:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.products = []\n\n    def add(self, product):\n        self.products.append(product)\n\n    def total_value(self):\n        return sum([p.price * p.qty for p in self.products])",
  "The Inventory object holds the list as an attribute and its methods loop over it. Callers use `inv.total_value()` instead of rewriting the loop.",
  "Every real system has a class like this: a cart, an asset register, a user directory. It's also where rules that involve several products belong."),
