T("Step 10: Fast lookups with a dictionary",
  "`find` loops over every product: O(n). Keeping a dictionary keyed by name makes it O(1), at the cost of updating the dictionary whenever a product is added.",
  "class Inventory:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.by_name = {}\n\n    def add(self, product):\n        self.by_name[product.name] = product\n\n    def find(self, name):\n        return self.by_name.get(name)   # None when missing",
  "A dictionary jumps straight to a key's value with a hash, instead of checking items one by one. `.get` returns None for a missing key instead of raising KeyError.",
  "Any lookup that runs often: asset tags, usernames, hostnames. Trading a little memory for speed like this is a standard interview answer."),
Q("With n products, how long does `find` take with the list loop, and with the dictionary?", ["O(n) with the list, O(1) with the dictionary", "O(1) both ways", "O(log n) both ways"], "O(n) with the list, O(1) with the dictionary",
  "The loop may check every product. The dictionary hashes the name and goes straight to it."),
